<p>German Chancellor Friedrich Merz arrived in Beijing on February 25 for his first trip to China since taking office. The two-day visit puts the spotlight on a relationship that is still vital for German business, but increasingly strained by trade frictions and intensifying industrial competition. Merz said it would be a mistake to “decouple” Germany from China, while pressing for fairer conditions as Chinese firms more often go head-to-head with German manufacturers. The trip is also a diplomatic balancing act. Merz is expected to meet Chinese leader Xi Jinping, while dealing with a tougher global trade climate shaped by President Trump’s tariff-driven push for concessions from trading partners and broader strains between Europe and Washington. Beyond economics, Germany plans to raise geopolitical issues, including China’s ties with Russia and questions around Ukraine, with Merz also seeking Chinese help in ending the war.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/nhzoimtPSjqpQlBtOSLAdFwX4SqU7QXi_4BzCU5laskreHykESP0DDhoxwqjR2Y08I86UWWToXpciR74WhXr6oWJc5a4qKxzSAHjCGvDhIgjnpWWkyvicjsARx1Vu961fWcUQrTrJxltn8DR0btz442opvxiA9jYA2VeuxzODMBfr_41gqZhGlHRk_HKUg7IoPnU-Mcb_cGPM7HqmiHaB5c2fHn85fpoZxTIMqzzdjvlyI_JpmAKKUPVLC71HQ' alt='German Chancellor Friedrich Merz arrives in Beijing on Feb. 25, 2026, beginning his first China trip as chancellor.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Scheduling detail: Merz is expected to meet Xi Jinping on Wednesday in talks framed as an effort to rebalance trade relations.</li><li>Industrial-policy pressure: Le Monde says China has shifted from a key driver of German exports to a direct rival, pushing Berlin to rethink its industrial policy.</li><li>Beijing’s outreach: The Hindu describes Merz as the latest in a parade of visiting leaders as China seeks support to push back against President Trump’s tariff-driven demands for concessions.</li><li>Agenda breadth (DW): DW says economics leads the agenda, but the visit also takes in Beijing’s relationships with Moscow and with Europe.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Chancellor Friedrich Merz: Merz argued Germany should not “decouple” from China even as it pursues fairer trade terms. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yjpke8072o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>German government agenda (DW framing): DW reports German economic interests lead the agenda, alongside discussions about Beijing’s relationships with Moscow and Europe. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/merz-in-china-german-chancellor-lands-in-beijing/live-76110529'>DW</a>)</li><li>China’s outreach amid tariffs: The Hindu describes Merz as part of a stream of visiting leaders as Beijing seeks support to push back against President Trump’s use of tariffs to demand concessions from partners. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/german-leader-friedrich-merz-in-china-to-press-for-fair-trade-help-ending-ukraine-war/article70674504.ece'>The Hindu</a>)</li><li>US–China–Europe tightrope: The New York Times says the trip will test Merz’s ability to balance Xi and President Trump at a moment of strain between Europe and Washington. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/world/europe/merz-germany-china-trip-beijing-xi.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/economy/article/2026/02/25/germany-is-being-forced-to-confront-its-growing-competition-with-china_6750849_19.html'>Germany is being forced to confront its growing competition with China</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/14ffb38d-498a-49a9-8b96-b7dc70f672c1'>Merz embarks on China trip aimed at rebalancing trade relations</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c5yjpke8072o'>German chancellor lands in Beijing for inaugural China trip</a> - bbc.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/02/24/world/europe/merz-germany-china-trip-beijing-xi.html'>Germany’s Leader Heads to China and Walks a Tightrope Between Xi and Trump</